The campaign did not newly expand to macOS: its earlier iterations already delivered the stealers to Macs in disk-image files, and the change Microsoft reported is the move to Terminal commands that fetch remote content and run a script-based loader ([Microsoft, 2026-05-06](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/06/clickfix-campaign-uses-fake-macos-utilities-lures-deliver-infostealers/)). Only the script campaign uses a one-line pipeline into the shell; the loader campaign runs a zsh loader through eval and the helper campaign downloads a Mach-O executable ([Microsoft, 2026-05-06](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/06/clickfix-campaign-uses-fake-macos-utilities-lures-deliver-infostealers/)). The wallet tampering replaces installed Ledger Wallet, Trezor Suite and Exodus apps with trojanized archives fetched from the C2, rather than shipping DMG copies of Ledger Live or Trezor Suite ([Microsoft, 2026-05-06](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/06/clickfix-campaign-uses-fake-macos-utilities-lures-deliver-infostealers/)). Detection rests on Microsoft's guidance rather than an Endpoint Security keychain hook: Terminal or shell sessions spawned by installers or user apps, unusual curl, base64, gunzip, osascript and dscl sequences, and archive creation followed by an HTTP POST ([Microsoft, 2026-05-06](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/06/clickfix-campaign-uses-fake-macos-utilities-lures-deliver-infostealers/)).
